## What it is

The everyday value types every loft program uses: whole numbers (`integer`), decimals (`float`), yes/no (`boolean`), and single characters (`character`).

## How it aids you

These are the building blocks you reach for constantly. Each variable says which one it holds, so the meaning of your data is clear and a slip like putting text where a number belongs is caught for you.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    count: integer = 42;
    ratio: float = 3.5;
    ok: boolean = true;
    letter: character = 'A';
    println("{count} {ratio} {ok} {letter}");   // 42 3.5 true A
}
```

## What it is

A growable list of values of one type. Add to it with `+=`, read an item by position (`v[0]`), ask its length with `len(v)`, and build a new list from an existing one with a comprehension.

## How it aids you

It is the go-to container for "a bunch of things" — a list you grow as you go and transform in a single line, without keeping track of the size yourself.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    v: vector<integer> = [1, 2, 3];
    v += [4];                              // grow
    doubled = [for x in v { x * 2 }];      // build a new list from it
    println("first={v[0]} len={len(v)} doubled_first={doubled[0]}");
    // first=1 len=4 doubled_first=2
}
```

## What it is

A collection of records that you look up by a key field. You give it a value for that field and it hands back the matching record straight away, with no searching through a list.

## How it aids you

When you need to find "the record with this name" over and over, a hash gives you the answer directly instead of scanning everything each time. You declare which field is the key, so every lookup reads naturally.

## Example

```loft
struct Person { name: text, age: integer }
struct Directory { people: hash<Person[name]> }
fn main() {
    dir = Directory { };
    dir.people = [ {name: "Ada", age: 36}, {name: "Bo", age: 20} ];
    dir.people += [ {name: "Cy", age: 51} ];
    println("Ada is {dir.people["Ada"].age}, Cy is {dir.people["Cy"].age}");
    // Ada is 36, Cy is 51
}
```

## What it is

A collection of records that stays in sorted order by a key field you choose. New records drop into the right position on their own, and looping over the collection visits them in that order. A leading `-` on the key means order it the other way round.

## How it aids you

You never have to re-sort by hand. Add records whenever you like and the collection is always in order — ideal for leaderboards, rankings, or anything you want to read back highest-first or lowest-first.

## Example

```loft
struct Item { name: text, score: integer }
struct Board { items: sorted<Item[-score]> }
fn main() {
    b = Board { items: [
        Item { name: "Ada", score: 30 },
        Item { name: "Bo", score: 90 },
        Item { name: "Cy", score: 60 }
    ] };
    for it in b.items {
        println("{it.name}: {it.score}");
    }
    // Bo: 90
    // Cy: 60
    // Ada: 30
}
```

## What it is

A collection of records kept in order by one or more key fields. You can jump straight to a record by its keys, and you can also walk through a whole ordered range of them. A leading `-` on a key orders it the other way round.

## How it aids you

It combines instant lookup with ordered iteration over more than one field at once — group by a primary key, break ties with a second, and read the records back in order.

## Example

```loft
struct Row { group: integer, name: text }
struct Db { rows: index<Row[group, name]> }
fn main() {
    db = Db { rows: [
        Row { group: 2, name: "b" },
        Row { group: 1, name: "a" },
        Row { group: 1, name: "c" }
    ] };
    for r in db.rows {
        println("{r.group}-{r.name}");
    }
    println("lookup 1,a -> {db.rows[1, "a"].group}");
    // 1-a
    // 1-c
    // 2-b
    // lookup 1,a -> 1
}
```

## What it is

A lazy sequence of values you can loop over one at a time. It produces each value on demand as the loop asks for it, instead of building a whole list up front. The `for` loops you write over ranges and vectors are powered by iterators.

## How it aids you

You can walk through a sequence — a range of numbers, the items of a collection — without ever building or storing the full list, so a loop over a million values costs no extra memory.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    total = 0;
    for i in 1..5 {          // steps through 1, 2, 3, 4 one at a time
        total += i;
    }
    println("total={total}");
    // total=10
}
```

## What it is

A fixed-size group of values that can each be a different type, written in parentheses like `(3, "three")`. You read the parts by position with `.0`, `.1`, and so on.

## How it aids you

When you want to pass or return a few related values together — a low and a high, or a code and a message — a tuple does it in one step, with no need to define a named type first.

## Example

```loft
fn min_max(a: integer, b: integer) -> (integer, integer) {
    if a <= b { (a, b) } else { (b, a) }
}
fn main() {
    pair: (integer, text) = (3, "three");
    println("{pair.0} = {pair.1}");
    (lo, hi) = min_max(9, 4);
    println("lo={lo} hi={hi}");
    // 3 = three
    // lo=4 hi=9
}
```

## What it is

A named type that groups related values together as fields. Create one by naming its fields; read a field with a dot.

## How it aids you

Instead of juggling several loose variables that belong together, you keep them in one named thing — easier to pass around, and the field names say what each value means.

## Example

```loft
struct Point { x: integer, y: integer }
fn main() {
    p = Point { x: 3, y: 4 };
    println("({p.x}, {p.y})");    // (3, 4)
}
```

## What it is

A type that is exactly one of a fixed set of named options.

## How it aids you

When something can only be one of a few choices, an enum names them all in one place — you can't use an invalid value, and a `match` can make sure you have handled every case.

## Example

```loft
enum Direction { North, East, South, West }
fn main() {
    d: Direction = East;
    label = match d { North | South => "vertical", East | West => "horizontal" };
    println(label);    // horizontal
}
```

## What it is

An enum whose choices each carry their own fields. One `Shape` type can be a `Circle` with a radius or a `Rect` with a width and height — every variant keeps exactly the data it needs.

## How it aids you

You keep a family of related shapes under one type, and you can give each variant its own version of a method. Calling `area()` runs the right one automatically, so your code stays simple no matter which variant it is holding.

## Example

```loft
enum Shape {
    Circle { radius: float },
    Rect { width: float, height: float }
}
fn area(self: Circle) -> float { 3.14 * self.radius * self.radius }
fn area(self: Rect) -> float { self.width * self.height }
fn main() {
    c = Circle { radius: 2.0 };
    r = Rect { width: 3.0, height: 4.0 };
    println("circle={c.area()} rect={r.area()}");
    // circle=12.56 rect=12
}
```

## What it is

When the type is already known — a variable's declared type, a function parameter, a return value — you can write a variant name on its own, like `East`, without repeating the enum name. Loft works out which enum it belongs to from the surrounding context.

## How it aids you

Your code reads cleaner: no need to spell out `Direction.East` every time when it is already clear a `Direction` is expected. It is shorter to write and easier to read, and the compiler still checks the name is a real variant of that enum.

## Example

```loft
enum Direction { North, East, South, West }
fn turn_around(d: Direction) -> Direction {
    match d { North => South, South => North, East => West, West => East }
}
fn main() {
    d: Direction = East;
    println("{turn_around(d)}");
    // West
}
```

## What it is

A function is a named piece of logic you can run whenever you like. You list what it takes in, say what it gives back, and write the steps once — then call it by name as often as you want. Mark a function `pub` to share it so other files can use it too.

## How it aids you

Write a calculation or task in one place and reuse it everywhere, instead of copying the same lines around. Each function has a clear name and a clear result, so your program reads as a list of intentions rather than a wall of detail — and `pub` lets you build a toolbox that other parts of your code can call.

## Example

```loft
pub fn square(n: integer) -> integer {
    n * n
}
fn greet(name: text) -> text {
    "Hello, {name}"
}
fn main() {
    println(greet("world"));               // Hello, world
    println("square(6) = {square(6)}");    // square(6) = 36
}
```

## What it is

Put `const` in front of a parameter's type to promise the function will never change that value. It is a guarantee written right in the signature, and Loft holds you to it — trying to change it is an error.

## How it aids you

Anyone reading the function knows at a glance that this input is safe: it goes in and comes back untouched. You can hand over your data without worrying it will be altered behind your back, and if the code ever tries to change it, you find out straight away instead of chasing a surprise later.

## Example

```loft
struct Account {
    balance: integer
}
fn describe(acct: const Account) -> text {
    "balance is {acct.balance}"
}
fn main() {
    a = Account { balance: 500 };
    println(describe(a));           // balance is 500
    println("still {a.balance}");   // still 500
}
```

## What it is

A method is a function that belongs to a value. Its first parameter is named `self`, which stands for the value you call it on, and you run it with a dot: `value.method()`.

## How it aids you

Related actions live together with the data they work on, so you write `circle.area()` instead of passing the circle into a loose function. Your code reads left to right like a sentence, and it is easy to find every action a value knows how to do.

## Example

```loft
struct Circle {
    radius: float
}
fn area(self: Circle) -> float {
    3.14159 * self.radius * self.radius
}
fn main() {
    c = Circle { radius: 2.0 };
    println("area = {c.area()}");    // area = 12.56636
}
```

## What it is

When a value can be one of several shapes — a Shape is either a Circle or a Rect — you write one version of a method for each shape, all with the same name. Loft runs the right one automatically, based on which shape the value actually holds.

## How it aids you

You call `shape.area()` and get the correct answer without checking which kind of shape it is yourself. Add a new shape later and you simply add its version of the method — the code that does the calling stays exactly the same.

## Example

```loft
enum Shape {
    Circle { radius: float },
    Rect { width: float, height: float }
}
fn area(self: Circle) -> float {
    3.14159 * self.radius * self.radius
}
fn area(self: Rect) -> float {
    self.width * self.height
}
fn main() {
    c = Circle { radius: 2.0 };
    r = Rect { width: 3.0, height: 4.0 };
    println("circle area = {c.area()}");   // circle area = 12.56636
    println("rect area = {r.area()}");     // rect area = 12
}
```

## What it is

Put `&` before a parameter's type to pass a variable by reference. The function then works on your actual variable, so any change it makes stays after the call returns. Without `&`, the function only sees a copy and your original is left alone.

## How it aids you

Let a function update your data in place — add to a running total, adjust a counter, fill in a field — without you having to catch a result and assign it back. It is the clear, visible way to say "this function will change what I hand it."

## Example

```loft
fn add_to(x: &integer, amount: integer) {
    x += amount;
}
fn main() {
    total = 10;
    add_to(total, 5);
    add_to(total, 100);
    println("total = {total}");    // total = 115
}
```

## What it is

A closure is a small anonymous function you can keep in a variable, hand to another function, or return. It remembers the values it uses from around it, taken at the moment you create it.

## How it aids you

Write a short piece of behaviour right where you need it — a callback, a quick rule, or a function that builds another function — without naming a separate function for each. Because it captures its values when it's made, it keeps behaving the same even if those values change afterwards.

## Example

```loft
fn make_adder(n: integer) -> fn(integer) -> integer {
    fn(x: integer) -> integer { n + x }
}
fn main() {
    add5 = make_adder(5);
    println("add5(10) = {add5(10)}");      // add5(10) = 15
    greeting = "Hello";
    greet = fn(name: text) -> text { "{greeting}, {name}" };
    greeting = "Bye";
    println(greet("world"));               // Hello, world
}
```

## What it is

A function's name can be used as a value all by itself. Store it in a variable, pass it to another function, or keep it for later — all without the parentheses that would run it right away.

## How it aids you

Choose behaviour while your program is running and pass it around like any other value: pick which action to apply, or hand a function to a helper like `map` to run over your data. Your code gets to decide not just what data to use but which action to take.

## Example

```loft
fn double(x: integer) -> integer {
    x * 2
}
fn main() {
    f = double;
    println("f(21) = {f(21)}");        // f(21) = 42
    doubled = map([1, 2, 3], double);
    println("{doubled}");              // [2,4,6]
}
```

## What it is

Three ready-made tools for working with a whole vector at once. `map` makes a new vector by transforming every element, `filter` keeps only the elements that pass a test, and `reduce` combines all the elements into a single value.

## How it aids you

Say what you want done to the list — double each one, keep the even ones, add them all up — instead of writing the loop and the bookkeeping by hand. It is shorter to write, harder to get wrong, and each line reads as one clear step.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    doubled = map(nums, |x| { x * 2 });
    evens = filter(nums, |x| { x % 2 == 0 });
    total = reduce(nums, 0, |acc, x| { acc + x });
    println("{doubled}");          // [2,4,6,8,10]
    println("{evens}");            // [2,4]
    println("total = {total}");    // total = 15
}
```

## What it is

A generic function uses a stand-in type, written `<T>`, so one function works for any type. Write "give me the first element of a vector" once, and it works for numbers, for words, or for anything else you pass in.

## How it aids you

Write a piece of logic a single time instead of keeping one near-identical copy per type. Loft fills in the real type for each call, so you get the convenience of one function together with the safety of exact type checking.

## Example

```loft
fn first<T>(v: vector<T>) -> T {
    v[0]
}
fn main() {
    ints = [10, 20, 30];
    words = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"];
    println("{first(ints)}");     // 10
    println("{first(words)}");    // alpha
}
```

## What it is

A bound like `<T: Ordered>` lets a generic function require that its type can do certain things — here, be compared with `<` and `>`. An interface names a group of abilities (Ordered for comparing, Addable for adding, and so on) that a type either has or does not.

## How it aids you

Write one function that works for every type that can be compared — numbers, text, and more — while Loft guarantees you only use abilities each type genuinely has. You get broad reuse without giving up the checks that catch mistakes early.

## Example

```loft
fn largest<T: Ordered>(a: T, b: T) -> T {
    if a > b { a } else { b }
}
fn main() {
    println("{largest(3, 7)}");        // 7
    println("{largest(2.5, 1.5)}");    // 2.5
    best = largest("apple", "pear");
    println(best);                     // pear
}
```

## What it is

`if` / `else` chooses between two branches — and it gives back a value, so you can assign the result directly.

## How it aids you

You set a variable to one thing or another in a single readable line, instead of declaring it first and reassigning it inside separate branches.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    x = 7;
    kind = if x % 2 == 0 { "even" } else { "odd" };
    println(kind);    // odd
}
```

## What it is

Repeat a block once for each item in a collection, or for each number in a range.

## How it aids you

Walk a list or count through a range without managing an index by hand — you name the current item and the loop hands you each one in turn.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    total = 0;
    for i in 1..=5 { total = total + i; }
    println("sum 1..=5 = {total}");    // sum 1..=5 = 15
}
```

## What it is

`match` picks one branch by comparing a value against a list of patterns, and gives back that branch's result. A pattern can be a specific value, a range, several options at once, or a catch-all. Every possibility has to be covered.

## How it aids you

One clear choice replaces a long chain of `if` / `else if`, and nothing can slip through uncovered. The chosen result drops straight into a variable instead of being set piece by piece across branches.

## Example

```loft
fn grade(score: integer) -> text {
    match score {
        null     => "absent",
        90..=100 => "A",
        80..90   => "B",
        _        => "other"
    }
}
fn main() {
    println(grade(95));  // A
    println(grade(85));  // B
    println(grade(50));  // other
}
```

## What it is

`is` tests whether an enum value is a particular variant — and, in the same step, can pull that variant's own fields out into named values you use right there.

## How it aids you

You check what a value is and reach its data in a single line, with no separate lookup or cast. Whatever you name in the braces is in scope inside the block, and only there.

## Example

```loft
enum Shape {
    Circle { radius: integer },
    Square { side: integer }
}

fn describe(s: Shape) -> text {
    if s is Circle { radius } {
        return "circle of radius {radius}";
    }
    if s is Square { side } {
        return "square of side {side}";
    }
    "unknown"
}

fn main() {
    println(describe(Circle { radius: 5 }));  // circle of radius 5
    println(describe(Square { side: 3 }));    // square of side 3
}
```

## What it is

`break` stops a loop early and `continue` skips straight to its next turn. To act on an outer loop from inside a nested one, name that loop's variable: `i#break` breaks the loop over `i`, and `i#continue` skips to its next turn.

## How it aids you

You leave a loop the moment you have what you need instead of running it to the end, and you can jump right out of several nested loops at once — no extra flags or bookkeeping.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    // continue: skip the value 3, add the rest
    sum = 0;
    for n in 1..=5 {
        if n == 3 { continue; }
        sum += n;
    }
    println("sum without 3: {sum}");                     // sum without 3: 12

    // break out of the OUTER loop from inside the inner one
    found = "";
    for i in 1..=3 {
        for j in 1..=3 {
            if i * j == 4 {
                found = "{i} and {j}";
                i#break;
            }
        }
    }
    println("first pair whose product is 4: {found}");   // first pair whose product is 4: 2 and 2
}
```

## What it is

You can make a type of your own work in a `for` loop by giving it a `next` method: it returns the following value each time it is asked, and `null` once there are no more.

## How it aids you

Your own types loop with the same `for x in thing` syntax as the built-in collections, so callers iterate over them without knowing or caring how each value is produced.

## Example

```loft
struct Countdown {
    n: integer
}

// Return the next value, or null when there is nothing left.
fn next(self: Countdown) -> integer {
    if self.n <= 0 { return null; }
    v = self.n;
    self.n = v - 1;
    v
}

fn main() {
    c = Countdown { n: 3 };
    for x in c {
        println("{x}");   // 3, then 2, then 1
    }
}
```

## What it is

Adding a `par(...)` clause to a `for` loop runs its work across several threads at once. Each item is handed to a worker function, and the loop body then sees that worker's result.

## How it aids you

Independent, repeated work finishes faster by using more than one processor core — with no manual thread handling. You write an ordinary loop and add one clause.

## Example

```loft
fn square(n: integer) -> integer {
    n * n
}

fn main() {
    // Each number is squared on a worker thread; b holds the result.
    total = 0;
    for a in 1..=5 par(b = square(a), 4) {
        total += b;
    }
    println("sum of squares 1..5: {total}");   // sum of squares 1..5: 55
}
```

## What it is

A generator is a function whose return type is `iterator<T>`. Instead of building a whole list up front, it hands back values one at a time with `yield`, pausing after each and resuming where it left off when the next value is asked for.

## How it aids you

You get a stream of values on demand — computed only as far as you actually consume it — and you drive it with the same `for` loop you use for any collection, with no intermediate list held in memory.

## Example

```loft
// A generator: its return type is iterator<T>, and it produces
// values one at a time with yield.
fn count_up(start: integer, stop: integer) -> iterator<integer> {
    for i in start..stop {
        yield i;
    }
}

fn main() {
    for n in count_up(1, 4) {
        println("{n}");   // 1, then 2, then 3
    }
}
```

## What it is

Text can carry values directly: write `{expr}` inside a string and its value is dropped in place. Strings in backticks may also span several lines, so the text you write looks like the text you get.

## How it aids you

Building messages stays simple and readable — no clumsy joining of pieces with `+`. And multi-line strings let you lay out an email, a template, or a block of output exactly as it should appear.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    name: text = "world";
    n: integer = 3;
    println("Hello, {name}. You have {n} messages.");
    note: text = `first line
second line mentions {name}`;
    println(note);
}
// Output:
// Hello, world. You have 3 messages.
// first line
// second line mentions world
```

## What it is

Inside `{...}` you can add a format after a colon to control how a value looks: pad it to a width, zero-fill it, fix the number of decimals, align it, or show it in hex, octal, or binary.

## How it aids you

You get tidy, aligned output — tables, reports, invoices, hex dumps — without hand-counting spaces or writing rounding code. The look of each value is described right next to it.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    n: integer = 255;
    pi: float = 3.14159;
    label: text = "hi";
    println("{n:#x} {n:05} {pi:6.2} {label:>6}");   // 0xff 00255   3.14     hi
}
```

## What it is

The usual operators, all built in: arithmetic (`+ - * / %` and `**` for powers), comparison (`== \!= < <= > >=`), logic (`and`, `or`, `\!`), and bit operations (`| & ^ << >>`).

## How it aids you

Everything you expect for calculating and comparing is there with familiar spelling and sensible precedence, so expressions read the way you would say them aloud — and one line can do real work.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    a: integer = 7;
    b: integer = 2;
    println("{a + b} {a - b} {a * b} {a / b} {a % b} {a ** b}");   // 9 5 14 3 1 49
    println("{a > b} {a == b} {a > 0 and b > 0} {a | b} {a & b}"); // true false true 7 2
}
```

## What it is

Read and write files on disk. Open a path with `file(...)`, `write(...)` text into it, read it back with `content()`, check whether a path `exists(...)`, and remove it with `delete(...)`. You can also list what is in a folder with `list_dir(...)`.

## How it aids you

Save results and load them back on the next run — settings, saved games, reports — with plain calls that take a path and hand you the contents, so your program remembers things between runs instead of starting from scratch every time.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    note = file("greeting.txt");
    note.write("hello from loft");
    reloaded = file("greeting.txt");
    println("read back: {reloaded.content()}");   // read back: hello from loft
    println("exists: {exists("greeting.txt")}");  // exists: true
    delete("greeting.txt");
    println("exists after delete: {exists("greeting.txt")}");  // exists after delete: false
}
```

## What it is

Read the settings the program was started with: environment variables via `env_variable(name)`, and the extra words typed after the program name on the command line via `arguments()`.

## How it aids you

Let the outside world shape a run without editing code — a value from the environment, a filename or option passed on the command line — so the same program adapts to each invocation.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    // run with:  GREET_NAME=world  loft greet.loft
    name = env_variable("GREET_NAME");
    println("hello, {name}");                         // hello, world
    println("extra arguments: {arguments().len()}");  // extra arguments: 0
}
```

## What it is

The everyday ways a program reports what it is doing: `print` and `println` write to the screen, the `log_info` / `log_warn` / `log_error` family records diagnostic notes to a log, `assert(condition, message)` stops with a message if the condition is false, and `panic(message)` stops right away.

## How it aids you

See what your program is doing and catch bad states early — a printed line while you develop, a logged note in a finished tool, an assertion that fails loudly the moment an assumption breaks — instead of chasing a silent wrong answer later.

## Example

```loft
fn main() {
    print("loading");
    print("...");
    println(" done");              // loading... done
    log_info("startup complete");  // recorded to the log when logging is configured
    steps = 3;
    assert(steps > 0, "need at least one step");
    println("ran {steps} steps");  // ran 3 steps
}
```

## What it is

`type Name = ...` gives an existing type a second, friendlier name. The alias and the original are the same type, so anywhere one works the other does too. It works for simple types, tuple types, and function types.

## How it aids you

Name a type for what it means in your program — `Celsius` instead of `float`, `Pair` instead of `(integer, text)` — so signatures read clearly and the intent is obvious at a glance.

## Example

```loft
type Celsius = float;
type Pair = (integer, text);

fn describe(temp: Celsius) -> text {
    "{temp} degrees"
}

fn main() {
    here: Celsius = 21.5;
    println(describe(here));         // 21.5 degrees
    basket: Pair = (3, "apples");
    println("{basket.0} {basket.1}");  // 3 apples
}
```
